      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) form an arithmetic progression, we need to ensure that the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Let's denote this common difference by \\(d\\).\n\nThe common difference \\(d\\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ d = a - 10 \\]\n\\[ d = b - a \\]\n\\[ d = ab - b \\]\n\nFrom these equations, we get:\n\\[ a - 10 = b - a \\]\n\\[ b - a = ab - b \\]\n\nSimplifying the first equation:\n\\[ a - 10 = b - a \\]\n\\[ 2a = b + 10 \\]\n\\[ b = 2a - 10 \\]\n\nSimplifying the second equation:\n\\[ b - a = ab - b \\]\n\\[ 2b - a = ab \\]\n\\[ 2(2a - 10) - a = (2a - 10)b \\]\n\\[ 4a - 20 - a = (2a - 10)b \\]\n\\[ 3a - 20 = (2a - 10)b \\]\n\nSince \\(b = 2a - 10\\), substitute \\(b\\) in the equation:\n\\[ 3a - 20 = (2a - 10)(2a - 10) \\]\n\\[ 3a - 20 = (2a - 10)^2 \\]\n\\[ 3a - 20 = 4a^2 - 40a + 100 \\]\n\\[ 4a^2 - 43a + 120 = 0 \\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 4\\), \\(b = -43\\), and \\(c = 120\\):\n\\[ a = \\frac{43 \\pm \\sqrt{(-43)^2 - 4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 120}}{2 \\cdot 4} \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{43 \\pm \\sqrt{1849 - 1920}}{8} \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{43 \\pm \\sqrt{-71}}{8} \\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt{-71}\\) is not a real number, the quadratic equation has no real solutions. Therefore, there are no real values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that satisfy the conditions of the arithmetic progression.\n\nThe number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
