      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) form an arithmetic progression, we start by using the property of arithmetic progressions. In an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Let this common difference be \\(d\\).\n\nThe terms of the arithmetic progression are:\n\\[10, a, b, ab.\\]\nThe common difference \\(d\\) must satisfy:\n\\[a - 10 = b - a = ab - b.\\]\n\nFrom the first two terms, we have:\n\\[a - 10 = b - a \\implies a + a = b + 10 \\implies 2a = b + 10 \\implies b = 2a - 10.\\]\n\nFrom the second and third terms, we have:\n\\[b - a = ab - b \\implies b - a = ab - b \\implies 2b - a = ab.\\]\nSubstituting \\(b = 2a - 10\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[2(2a - 10) - a = a(2a - 10) \\implies 4a - 20 - a = 2a^2 - 10a \\implies 3a - 20 = 2a^2 - 10a \\implies 2a^2 - 13a + 20 = 0.\\]\n\nWe solve the quadratic equation \\(2a^2 - 13a + 20 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -13\\), and \\(c = 20\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{(-13)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 20}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{169 - 160}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm \\sqrt{9}}{4} = \\frac{13 \\pm 3}{4}.\\]\nThis gives us two solutions for \\(a\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{13 + 3}{4} = \\frac{16}{4} = 4,\\]\n\\[a = \\frac{13 - 3}{4} = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2}.\\]\n\nFor \\(a = 4\\), we find \\(b\\):\n\\[b = 2a - 10 = 2 \\cdot 4 - 10 = 8 - 10 = -2.\\]\nSo, one pair is \\((4, -2)\\).\n\nFor \\(a = \\frac{5}{2}\\), we find \\(b\\):\n\\[b = 2a - 10 = 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} - 10 = 5 - 10 = -5.\\]\nSo, another pair is \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, -5\\right)\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
