      "text": "Given the arithmetic progression \\(10, a, b, ab\\), we know that the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Therefore, we can write the following equations based on the property of arithmetic progressions:\n\n\\[\na - 10 = b - a\n\\]\n\\[\nb - a = ab - b\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's solve the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\na - 10 = b - a \\implies 2a = b + 10 \\implies a = \\frac{b + 10}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{b + 10}{2}\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[\nb - \\frac{b + 10}{2} = ab - b\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\nb - \\frac{b + 10}{2} = \\frac{2b - (b + 10)}{2} = \\frac{2b - b - 10}{2} = \\frac{b - 10}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b - 10}{2} = \\left(\\frac{b + 10}{2}\\right)b - b\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b - 10}{2} = \\frac{b^2 + 10b}{2} - b = \\frac{b^2 + 10b - 2b}{2} = \\frac{b^2 + 8b}{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by 2 to clear the denominators:\n\n\\[\nb - 10 = \\frac{b^2 + 8b}{2}\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by 2 again to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\n2(b - 10) = b^2 + 8b \\implies 2b - 20 = b^2 + 8b \\implies b^2 + 8b - 2b + 20 = 0 \\implies b^2 + 6b + 20 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve the quadratic equation \\(b^2 + 6b + 20 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\), where \\(A = 1\\), \\(B = 6\\), and \\(C = 20\\):\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{6^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 20}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 80}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{-44}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 2i\\sqrt{11}}{2} = -3 \\pm i\\sqrt{11}\n\\]\n\nSince the solutions \\(b = -3 + i\\sqrt{11}\\) and \\(b = -3 - i\\sqrt{11}\\) are complex, they are not real numbers. Therefore, there are no real values of \\(b\\) that satisfy the equation, and consequently, there are no real pairs \\((a, b)\\) such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) form an arithmetic progression.\n\nThe number of such pairs is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).\n\nCongratulations! You've successfully determined that the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
